---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
	(entry) => entry.slug === "ship-motion-laws-2026-11-04",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Build choices must become flight character</h2>
		<p>
			A designer adds equipment to a ship, doubles its engines, or angles a pair
			of thrusters. Each choice should create an understandable change in how
			the ship gains speed, turns, carries momentum and recovers. Those changes
			must leave room for different useful ships: a powerful launch need not
			mean the highest cruising speed, and a heavy ship need not be merely a
			worse light one. The current mechanics work, but the owner finds their
			range and depth insufficient for developing the game's ship design.
		</p>
		<p>
			The tension is between expressive construction and readable control. If
			every engine arrangement needs its own exception, designers cannot reason
			about new builds. If one physically elaborate rule makes normal turns and
			recovery laborious, the game loses its arcade character. Void Drift needs
			a small, coherent set of relationships that can produce varied ships while
			keeping two-control flight understandable.
		</p>
		<p>
			This cycle asks which shared rule should translate an assembled ship and
			pilot input into linear and rotational motion. Begin with controlled
			flight without gravity, then require that the same rule remains coherent
			through the existing gravity laws. The outcome is a flight-law decision
			with demonstrated tradeoffs, not final fleet balance. A rule must explain
			both what extra machinery buys and what the pilot gives up.
		</p>

		<h3>01.1 / The first decision and its boundaries</h3>
		<p>
			Settle the minimum common law for mass, thrust composition, speed and
			turning. “Common” means consistent relationships and explicit component
			properties; it does not require one equation, identical engine response,
			or the removal of every intentional hull distinction. Existing ships are
			reference cases, not numerical targets that all alternatives must
			reproduce.
		</p>
		<p>
			The working design hypothesis is that these relationships can explain
			useful differences between builds without accumulating hull-specific
			corrections. It fails if the rule works only for a symmetric example,
			needs hidden compensation to make ordinary layouts fly, or offers variety
			that pilots cannot understand or recover from. No motion law has yet been
			selected.
		</p>

		<h3>01.2 / The questions the law must answer</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Mass and inertia.</strong>
			What should added equipment cost in acceleration, braking, turn-in and
			stopping a turn? Does mass also change attainable engine-driven speed?
			Does placement matter separately from total mass? A moving centre of mass
			and component-derived rotational inertia are options to justify, not
			prerequisites. Engine additions must include their added mass when judging
			their benefit.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>One engine and many.</strong>
			What determines an engine's force at rest, while already moving, and when
			opposing motion? How do aligned, angled, opposed and mixed-family engines
			combine? Separate useful acceleration from eventual speed, and separate
			commanded thrust from thrust actually delivered. Pulse and charge
			behaviour may remain distinct without giving each family an incompatible
			composition rule.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Speed and momentum.</strong>
			Distinguish speed attainable under sustained propulsion from instantaneous
			speed, directional thrust effectiveness and any absolute simulation limit.
			State the reference frame. What happens after release, after turning
			across existing momentum, and above the engine-driven range? Whether extra
			engines raise that range, and whether gains diminish, must be decided
			before selecting a curve.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Turning and stabilization.</strong>
			Explain how engine angle and lever arm affect translation and rotation,
			individually and together. What do left, right, both and neither command?
			What can stabilizers actually counteract, how quickly, and with what
			translation or thrust cost? Include insufficient or absent stabilization
			where the build permits it. Whether forward speed changes turning
			authority is an explicit choice. Do not silently add another player
			control or unlimited corrective torque.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>External acceleration.</strong>
			A well must not be mistaken for another engine. Explain what propulsion
			and stabilization can do while gravity changes the ship's velocity, and
			what momentum remains on exit. Decide whether excess speed persists or
			decays and why that behaviour serves play. A high peak near a body alone
			is not evidence of a useful exit or a controllable manoeuvre.
		</p>

		<h3>01.3 / Evidence that would make the decision credible</h3>
		<p>
			Start with a small contrasting set of real builds: a light craft, a
			heavier craft and an angled or asymmetric propulsion case. Compare one
			meaningful change at a time: extra non-propulsive mass; extra identical
			engines with their mass; a mixed engine set; a changed angle or lever; and
			changed stabilization. Use controlled fixtures only where production
			loadouts cannot express a necessary case, and label them as such.
		</p>
		<p>
			For each candidate, explain the predicted tradeoff before looking at
			paths. Observe acceleration, speed over time, turn rate, turn stopping
			time, heading versus travel direction and recovery distance. Use world
			units and seconds, so camera zoom cannot masquerade as a handling change.
			Time to a stated fraction of cruise speed is useful only when that
			candidate has a defined cruise speed; otherwise compare a common speed or
			fixed-duration burn. Numerical thresholds remain to be established through
			exploration rather than inherited from the supplied proposal.
		</p>
		<p>
			Repeat matched flights with no well and with one configured well, holding
			build, initial state and input sequence fixed. Distinguish coasting from
			powered passes and contacts from successful flybys. Record the law, well
			settings, power mode and elapsed time. Include release, reversal,
			both-input flight, weak stabilization and repeated turning at high speed:
			a claimed speed bound must survive changes in heading and engine mix.
		</p>
		<p>
			A satisfying result explains the direction of each change, supports at
			least two useful handling characters, and keeps its stated guarantees when
			forces compose. Replays establish reproducibility and numerical behaviour;
			hands-on flight establishes whether control is readable and recovery
			satisfying. The designer judges those qualities. Curves alone cannot
			establish fun, combat balance or a winner.
		</p>

		<h3>01.4 / Existing means and the missing experiment</h3>
		<p>
			The current build-flight calculation already uses total fitted mass,
			angled thrust and torque, persistent angular velocity and braking
			stabilizers. Ion thrust aggregates with diminishing speed gains; other
			families retain their own speed limits and delivery timing. Rotational
			inertia uses a chassis factor times mass, and some hulls have explicit
			drive rules. This provides a working baseline, not evidence that its
			particular limits are the desired future law.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/instrument">Ship tuning</a>
			compares editable builds under fixed burns with supplied power, without
			gravity. <a href="/lab/gravity-tuning">Gravity tuning</a> already adds one
			mass-based well and a no-well reference through the shared calculation,
			but keeps its ships fixed. It supports inverse and inverse-square falloff,
			a pull cut-off and body contact. Use these current laws; choosing new
			gravity constants is a separate decision.
		</p>
		<p>
			The missing capability is comparing candidate ship laws and controlled
			build changes in that same well experiment. Mass, geometry and candidate
			law selection are not ordinary instrument settings today. Keep proposed
			extensions in this study; update the instrument specification when they
			are approved. Reuse the shared production simulation for comparisons. The
			initial mathematical comparison can proceed without this extension, but
			gravity compatibility cannot be declared demonstrated without it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Builder test flight currently removes planets, retains a small sun and
			still accounts for power. A later hands-on trial needs a repeatable planet
			setup and an explicit unlimited-energy option—the game's equivalent of
			“unlimited fuel”—to isolate handling from endurance. That option should
			supply power without erasing engine response timing or changing mass.
			Normal-power flight is a subsequent integration check. These are evidence
			needs, not tool features implemented or approved by this problem
			statement.
		</p>

		<h3>01.5 / The dependency order</h3>
		<p>
			First resolve propulsion and rotation in empty space with supplied power:
			what a build does, why it does it, and what the two controls request.
			Treat translation and rotation together where angled engines couple them;
			a straight-line speed curve alone cannot settle this first problem. Then
			test the surviving rule through a fixed gravity well and hands-on flight.
			If it fails there, reopen the specific motion assumption rather than
			compensate by retuning the well.
		</p>
		<p>
			Engine-family identities and fleet tuning follow the common law. Energy
			costs and endurance follow demonstrated handling. Builder performance
			indicators follow quantities the law can truthfully predict. Combat
			balance, component destruction, freely placed components and optional
			flight-computer upgrades are adjacent design problems, not requirements
			for this cycle. Their relevance can be recorded without implementing them.
		</p>

		<h3>01.6 / How to use the supplied proposal</h3>
		<p>
			The owner's deliberately naive-agent PDF contributes possibilities:
			mass-dependent speed, diminishing engine returns, directional force taper,
			coupled rotation, stabilization and gravity overspeed. Its Double-Diamond
			instructions do not govern this study. Its equations, physical analogies,
			claims about other games, engine archetypes and numerical targets are
			unvalidated hypotheses, not adopted project facts. In particular, an
			“exhaust ceiling,” “structural impedance” or an overspeed wake must earn
			its place as a game rule; the name is no evidence.
		</p>
		<p>
			Exploration should compare coherent alternatives against the current
			baseline, not merely tune the PDF's preferred formula. It must state which
			parts of a candidate are physical modelling, deliberate arcade rules or
			control assistance. No particular logarithm, drag law, centre of mass
			model or stabilization policy is selected here.
		</p>
		<p>
			Implementation references:
			<code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/build-flight.ts</code>,
			<code>schemas/loadout.ts</code>
			and <code>schemas/ion.ts</code> in that library;
			<code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/simulation.ts</code>;
			<code>packages/core/src/lib/physics/Physics.ts</code>. Owning contracts:
			<code>docs/specs/motion-instrument.md</code>,
			<code>docs/specs/gravity-tuning.md</code>
			and
			<code>docs/specs/gravity-field.md</code>. This page records the problem;
			production behaviour and standing design principles are unchanged.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>02 / Exploration</h2>
	</section>
	<details>
		<summary>Earlier instrument capability proposals</summary>
		<p>
			These notes were moved from the Motion instrument spec. They preserve
			earlier investigation needs, not approved instrument requirements. Current
			study decisions take precedence.
		</p>
		<p>
			Problem framing: Ship motion: a law for assembled ships, part 01. This is
			an unimplemented investigation need, not a change to the current
			instrument contract or authorization to build it.
		</p>
		<p>
			The study needs matched comparisons of candidate propulsion/rotation laws
			and controlled mass or propulsion-geometry changes, first without gravity
			and then with one configured production mass well. Existing Ship tuning
			cannot express all these changes; Gravity tuning shares its calculation
			but fixes the ships. Resolve this boundary explicitly before implementing
			a combined experiment. Reuse the shared calculation and views, retain the
			current baseline, and record the candidate law, build, initial state, well
			and power conditions for replay. Any additional burn or measurement needed
			to expose a claimed behaviour belongs in the shared instrument contract,
			not a study-local motion chart. The exact extension is left to exploration
			and subsequent readiness/scope.
		</p>
		<p>
			Exploration 02.1 (hull speed ratings) identifies three specific
			capabilities:
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Hull speed rating.</strong>
			A per-vehicle value naming the hull&#x27;s engine-driven cruise, applied
			to every engine family and independent of combined thrust. Today it can
			only be emulated for all-ion builds, by setting the ion speed ceiling to
			the rating and the heavy-ion bonus to zero; thrust weaker than one light
			ion then still receives a proportionally lower ceiling, and fusion and
			plasma keep their own limits.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Sustained straight burn.</strong>
			A fixed program holding both controls for the full 14 s, so a slow climb
			finishes before any turn. B2 turns at 6 s, before a loaded Wasp reaches
			90% of its rating.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Predicted curve.</strong>
			The candidate law&#x27;s analytical speed curve drawn by the instrument
			beside each simulated trace, distinguishable from it, so a study separates
			prediction from observation without its own chart.
		</p>
	</details>
	<section aria-labelledby="additive-instrument-proposal">
		<h3 id="additive-instrument-proposal">
			Instrument capability proposal: additive build speed
		</h3>
		<p>
			Exploration 02.2 demonstrates an additive all-ion build speed by emulating
			each vehicle&#x27;s calculated cruise through the existing ion
			<code>maxSpeed</code>
			setting and a zero combined-thrust bonus. This makes the proposed build
			value visible in the production flight calculation, but the configuration
			cannot record its sources. For a later mixed-family or angled-mount
			decision, a configured comparison may need explicit hull base speed,
			per-engine-family speed contributions, and the candidate composition rule,
			with the instrument showing the derived build speed alongside the
			simulation. This is an investigation capability, not an approved
			instrument contract change; decide whether it is needed after that
			question is framed.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
